-- Retired: entity_type_counts_approx(), partition_pressure(text),
-- index_usage_report(bigint) and index_usage_detail(text, integer).
--
-- These four shipped installed with ZERO callers — not from SQL, not from the extension C,
-- not from production C#. g4_dead_canonical caught that on merge and turned main red, which
-- is the gate working exactly as designed: its ceiling sits at the count it was frozen at,
-- meaning no further uncalled surface, and these were four more.
--
-- WHY A DROP MODULE AND NOT JUST DELETING THE .sql.in FILES. Removing a file from the
-- manifests stops FUTURE installs from creating the function. It does nothing to a database
-- that already ran an upgrade carrying it — ALTER EXTENSION ... UPDATE has no mechanism to
-- retract a CREATE that a previous version performed. Those catalogs would keep all four
-- indefinitely, and the gate would never flag them again because the gate scans SOURCE, not
-- pg_proc. The result is uncalled surface that has become invisible: strictly worse than the
-- state that failed CI, because nothing is left pointing at it.
--
-- These are not deleted ideas. entity_type_counts_approx measured the 15,150,442 distinct
-- Chess_Position figure in ~20ms where the exact variant times out, partition_pressure is the
-- op that would have surfaced the 48.81% Hilbert skew years earlier, and index_usage_report
-- makes 64 GB of never-scanned index a substrate fact. They come back when something calls
-- them — consensus_partition_pressure (functions/inspect/) is the natural first caller, since
-- partition_pressure was written to generalize exactly what it does by hand today. A drop is
-- not a verdict on the instrument; it is a refusal to keep installed surface that nothing
-- reaches.
--
-- IF EXISTS throughout: a fresh install never created these, and this module runs there too.
DROP FUNCTION IF EXISTS entity_type_counts_approx();
DROP FUNCTION IF EXISTS partition_pressure(text);
DROP FUNCTION IF EXISTS index_usage_report(bigint);
DROP FUNCTION IF EXISTS index_usage_detail(text, integer);
